  {
    "title": "Final voiced consonants may stay voiced or harden",
    "body": "At the end of a word, b, d, g, z, ž, v may be said as written or hardened to p, t, k, s, š, f, as most Slavs do. Both are correct (also prosba as “PROZ-ba”); staying close to the spelling is recommended.",
    "pattern": "(?<=\\p{L})(?:[bdgzžvđ]|dž)(?!\\p{L})",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "god",
        "say": "god (or got)",
        "en": "year"
      },
      {
        "word": "grad",
        "say": "grad (or grat)",
        "en": "city"
      },
      {
        "word": "sněg",
        "say": "snyeg (or snyek)",
        "en": "snow"
      },
      {
        "word": "obed",
        "say": "O-bed (or O-bet)",
        "en": "lunch"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "lj and nj are soft “ly” and “ny” sounds",
    "body": "lj is one soft sound like “lli” in “million”, and nj like “ny” in “canyon”. In nouns ending in -nje (plavanje, rěšenje) you may also say n and j separately.",
    "pattern": "[ln]j",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "ljudi",
        "say": "LYOO-dee",
        "en": "people"
      },
