  {
    "title": "r between consonants acts as a vowel: “uhr”",
    "body": "After a consonant and not followed by a vowel, r forms a syllable of its own. Say it with a short “uh” before it, a bit like the “ur” in “Murphy” but with a rolled r.",
    "pattern": "(?<=[bcčćdđfghklmnpsštvzž])r(?![aeiouyě])",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "prvy",
        "say": "PUHR-vih",
        "en": "first"
      },
      {
        "word": "vrlo",
        "say": "VUHR-lo",
        "en": "very"
      },
      {
        "word": "četvrtok",
        "say": "CHE-tvuhr-tok",
        "en": "Thursday"
      },
      {
        "word": "trg",
        "say": "tuhrg",
        "en": "square, market"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ć sounds like “ch” in “cheap”, or simply like č",
    "body": "ć is a softer “ch”, as in “cheap” said with the lips spread, like Croatian ć or Polish ć. Saying it exactly like č is equally correct.",
    "pattern": "ć",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "noć",
        "say": "noch",
        "en": "night"
      },
      {
        "word": "plaća",
